              <p class="trap-help" id="trap-density-help">Starts at <a href="https://dressertraprock.com/price-schedule/">Dresser Trap Rock’s listed 1.3 US short tons per cubic yard</a>. <a href="https://shop.ficksupply.com/product/trap-small-34-1">Fick lists 2,700 lb/cu yd</a> for its 3/4–1 in product. Your material may differ.</p>
            </div>
            <p class="trap-error" id="trap-error" role="alert"></p>
          </div>
          <div class="trap-panel trap-results">
            <p class="trap-eyebrow">Installed layer</p>
            <div class="trap-primary"><output id="trap-finished-yards">0.93</output><span>cu yd</span></div>
            <p class="trap-result-note"><output id="trap-finished-feet">25.00</output> cu ft before optional extra</p>
            <div class="trap-result-grid">
              <div><span>Planned bulk order</span><strong><output id="trap-order-yards">0.93</output> cu yd</strong></div>
              <div><span>Estimated US short tons</span><strong><output id="trap-order-tons">1.20</output> tons</strong></div>
              <div><span>One ton covers at this depth</span><strong><output id="trap-ton-coverage">83.1</output> sq ft</strong></div>
              <div><span>Measured area</span><strong><output id="trap-measured-area">100</output> sq ft</strong></div>
            </div>
            <p class="trap-result-note">The ton figure estimates loose material from the weight entered. Ask whether your supplier sells by measured tons or bucket volume, and whether delivery has a minimum.</p>
            <p class="trap-sr-only" id="trap-result-summary" aria-live="polite"></p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="trap-reverse">
          <div>
            <p class="trap-eyebrow">Already have a quote?</p>
            <h3>How far will those tons go?</h3>
            <p>Use the same depth and supplier weight above to estimate the geometric area for material offered by the ton.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="trap-reverse-controls">
            <label for="trap-known-tons">Available short tons</label>
            <input id="trap-known-tons" type="number" min="0.01" max="100000" step="any" inputmode="decimal" value="1" />
            <strong><output id="trap-known-coverage">83.1</output> sq ft</strong>
            <p id="trap-reverse-error" class="trap-error" role="status"></p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <article class="trap-guide">
      <div class="container">
        <section class="trap-feature" aria-labelledby="trap-example-title">
          <div>
            <p class="trap-eyebrow">The calculation</p>
            <h2 id="trap-example-title">A 20 × 5 ft area at 3 inches</h2>
            <p>Measure only the part that will receive trap rock. This 100 sq ft layer is <strong>25 cu ft</strong> (100 × 3 ÷ 12) or <strong>0.93 cu yd</strong> (25 ÷ 27). Using <a href="https://dressertraprock.com/price-schedule/">Dresser’s 2,600 lb/cu yd average</a>, its estimated loose ordering weight is <strong>2,407 lb, or 1.20 US short tons</strong>. The volume is the same if another supplier’s product weighs more or less.</p>
            <div class="trap-formula" aria-label="Trap rock quantity formulas">
              <p><strong>Finished cu ft</strong> = area (sq ft) × finished depth (in) ÷ 12</p>
              <p><strong>Planned cu yd</strong> = finished cu ft × (1 + optional extra ÷ 100) ÷ 27</p>
              <p><strong>US short tons</strong> = planned cu yd × supplier lb/cu yd ÷ 2,000</p>
            </div>
            <p>Supplier coverage suggestions are useful for shopping, but a range such as “2–3 inches” cannot yield one exact square-foot figure. Choose a finished depth first, then compare the estimate with the yard’s product guidance.</p>
          </div>
          <figure class="trap-figure">
            <div class="trap-figure-scroll" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll trap rock quantity diagram"><img src="/trap-rock-layer-calculation.svg" width="760" height="500" loading="lazy" alt="A measured 20 by 5 foot trap rock area with a 3 inch finished layer becomes 25 cubic feet, 0.93 cubic yard, and about 1.20 short tons at 2,600 pounds per cubic yard." /></div>
            <figcaption>The layer dimensions fix the volume; the supplier’s loose bulk weight converts that volume to tons. <span>Swipe for the full diagram on a narrow screen.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="trap-card" aria-labelledby="trap-grade-title">
          <p class="trap-eyebrow">Name the product</p>
          <h2 id="trap-grade-title">“Trap rock” is a rock type, not one gradation</h2>
          <p><a href="https://pubs.usgs.gov/ha/ha730/ch_l/L-text4.html">USGS groups basalt and diabase under trap rock</a>. A quarry then crushes and screens that rock into different products. <a href="https://dressertraprock.com/price-schedule/">Dresser’s current schedule</a> separately lists 3/4 in rock, 3/4 in Class 5 base, and drain-field stone. Ask for the product name and size range, not only the geological name.</p>
          <div class="trap-grade-grid">
            <div class="trap-grade-item"><span>01 / CLEAN</span><h3>Open-graded crushed rock</h3><p>Little fine material leaves spaces between pieces. Check the actual product specification for a <a href="/drain-rock-calculator">drain trench</a>, an open-graded base, or a contained decorative surface. It may move under tires if used as an unsupported driveway wearing course.</p></div>
            <div class="trap-grade-item"><span>02 / GRADED</span><h3>Minus or Class 5 base</h3><p>A range of coarse and fine particles is intended to compact into a firmer base. <a href="https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/publications/research/infrastructure/pavements/97148/073.cfm">FHWA distinguishes dense-graded structural bases from open-graded drainage layers</a>. Ask the yard for its compaction guidance and loose delivery weight.</p></div>
            <div class="trap-grade-item"><span>03 / LARGE</span><h3>Drain-field or erosion stone</h3><p>Larger screened rock serves a different design. Dresser lists a 1–4 in drain-field product and still larger erosion sizes. Use the project specification for size and thickness; a 3 in decorative-layer example should not be copied to those applications.</p></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="trap-card" aria-labelledby="trap-table-title">
          <p class="trap-eyebrow">Reference</p>
          <h2 id="trap-table-title">Coverage changes with finished depth</h2>
          <p>These are geometric yields at the <strong>2,600 lb/cu yd example weight</strong>, before any order extra. One cubic yard always contains 27 cu ft; the square feet per short ton depend on bulk weight. For another product, change the calculator field.</p>
          <div class="trap-table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Finished depth</th><th scope="col">Sq ft per cu yd</th><th scope="col">Sq ft per short ton</th><th scope="col">100 sq ft needs</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">2 in</th><td>162</td><td>124.6</td><td>0.62 cu yd / 0.80 ton</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">3 in</th><td>108</td><td>83.1</td><td>0.93 cu yd / 1.20 tons</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">4 in</th><td>81</td><td>62.3</td><td>1.23 cu yd / 1.60 tons</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="trap-small">Rounded table values are for estimating. Check the chosen stone size against the layer depth; larger rock may need a deeper layer or a different construction detail.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="trap-buying" aria-labelledby="trap-buy-title">
          <div>
            <p class="trap-eyebrow">Before you call the quarry</p>
            <h2 id="trap-buy-title">Four details that make a useful quote</h2>
            <p>Bring a material takeoff that a supplier can verify. It is especially useful when the same yard stocks clean decorative trap, compactable base, and larger drainage or erosion grades.</p>
          </div>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>State the use and grade.</strong> A driveway base, visible stone bed, and pipe trench call for different gradations. Ask for the full product name and size range.</li>
            <li><strong>Give area and finished depth.</strong> Measure the actual footprint, deduct large features, and note low spots separately. See the <a href="/rock-depth-guide">depth guide</a> for layer planning.</li>
            <li><strong>Confirm units and weight.</strong> Ask whether the quote is by cubic yard or US short ton and what loose weight per cubic yard they use for that load. A truck scale ticket is a delivered weight; it does not prove a universal density.</li>
            <li><strong>Check delivery and placement.</strong> Confirm the minimum order, drop location, access, and whether the grade you chose is currently available. Keep clean drain stone separate from any base containing fines.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="trap-card trap-sources" aria-labelledby="trap-sources-title">
          <h2 id="trap-sources-title">Source notes and practical limits</h2>
          <p>The <a href="https://dressertraprock.com/price-schedule/">Dresser Trap Rock 2026 product schedule</a> supplies the 1.3-short-ton/cu-yd starting value and shows separate gradations. <a href="https://shop.ficksupply.com/product/trap-small-34-1">Fick’s 3/4–1 in listing</a> uses 2,700 lb/cu yd, illustrating why the field is editable. The <a href="https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/publications/research/infrastructure/pavements/97148/073.cfm">FHWA granular-base guide</a> explains how grading and fines affect base stability and drainage. The calculator estimates material volume; it cannot select a structural pavement design or predict the exact weight of a wet, graded delivery.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="trap-card" aria-labelledby="trap-faq-title">
          <h2 id="trap-faq-title">Trap rock questions</h2>
          <div class="trap-faq-grid">
            {faqItems.map(item => <div><h3>{item.q}</h3><p>{item.a}</p></div>)}
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </article>

    <TopicClusterNav upLinks={upLinks} lateralLinks={lateralLinks} bridgeLinks={bridgeLinks} supportGuides={supportGuides} />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  function initTrapCalculator() {
    const byId = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id);
    const modes = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLInputElement>('input[name="trap-mode"]');
    const length = byId('trap-length') as HTMLInputElement;
    const width = byId('trap-width') as HTMLInputElement;
    const areaInput = byId('trap-area') as HTMLInputElement;
    const depth = byId('trap-depth') as HTMLInputElement;
    const extra = byId('trap-extra') as HTMLInputElement;
    const density = byId('trap-density') as HTMLInputElement;
    const knownTons = byId('trap-known-tons') as HTMLInputElement;
    const error = byId('trap-error') as HTMLElement;
    const reverseError = byId('trap-reverse-error') as HTMLElement;
    const dimensionsFields = byId('trap-dimensions-fields') as HTMLElement;
    const areaFields = byId('trap-area-fields') as HTMLElement;
    const primaryOutputs = ['trap-finished-yards', 'trap-finished-feet', 'trap-order-yards', 'trap-order-tons', 'trap-ton-coverage', 'trap-measured-area'];

    function valid(input: HTMLInputElement, min: number, max: number) {
      const value = Number(input.value);
      const ok = input.value.trim() !== '' && Number.isFinite(value) && value >= min && value <= max;
      input.setAttribute('aria-invalid', String(!ok));
      return ok ? value : null;
    }
    function output(id: string, value: string) {
      const target = byId(id);
      if (target) target.textContent = value;
    }
    function clearPrimary() {
      primaryOutputs.forEach(id => output(id, '—'));
      output('trap-result-summary', '');
    }
    function calculate() {
      const mode = document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('input[name="trap-mode"]:checked')?.value || 'dimensions';
      dimensionsFields.hidden = mode !== 'dimensions';
      areaFields.hidden = mode !== 'area';
      const depthValue = valid(depth, 0.25, 24);
      const extraValue = valid(extra, 0, 100);
      const densityValue = valid(density, 100, 10000);
      let area: number | null;
      if (mode === 'dimensions') {
        const lengthValue = valid(length, 0.01, 10000);
        const widthValue = valid(width, 0.01, 10000);
        areaInput.removeAttribute('aria-invalid');
        area = lengthValue !== null && widthValue !== null ? lengthValue * widthValue : null;
      } else {
        area = valid(areaInput, 0.01, 10000000);
        length.removeAttribute('aria-invalid');
        width.removeAttribute('aria-invalid');
      }
      if (area === null || area > 10000000 || depthValue === null) {
        error.textContent = 'Enter a positive area and a finished depth from 0.25 to 24 inches.';
        clearPrimary();
      } else {
        const finishedFeet = area * depthValue / 12;
        const finishedYards = finishedFeet / 27;
        output('trap-finished-feet', finishedFeet.toFixed(2));
        output('trap-finished-yards', finishedYards.toFixed(2));
        output('trap-measured-area', area.toLocaleString('en-US', { maximumFractionDigits: 1 }));
        const plannedYards = extraValue === null ? null : finishedYards * (1 + extraValue / 100);
        const plannedTons = plannedYards === null || densityValue === null ? null : plannedYards * densityValue / 2000;
        output('trap-order-yards', plannedYards === null ? '—' : plannedYards.toFixed(2));
        output('trap-order-tons', plannedTons === null ? '—' : plannedTons.toFixed(2));
        output('trap-ton-coverage', densityValue === null ? '—' : (2000 / densityValue * 324 / depthValue).toFixed(1));
        error.textContent = extraValue === null ? 'Enter an optional extra from 0 to 100% to plan an order.' : densityValue === null ? 'Enter a supplier bulk weight from 100 to 10,000 lb/cu yd for tons and one-ton coverage.' : '';
        output('trap-result-summary', plannedYards === null ? `${finishedYards.toFixed(2)} cubic yards installed for ${area.toFixed(1)} square feet.` : plannedTons === null ? `${plannedYards.toFixed(2)} cubic yards planned for ${area.toFixed(1)} square feet.` : `${plannedYards.toFixed(2)} cubic yards and ${plannedTons.toFixed(2)} short tons planned for ${area.toFixed(1)} square feet.`);
      }
      const known = valid(knownTons, 0.01, 100000);
      if (known === null || depthValue === null || densityValue === null) {
        output('trap-known-coverage', '—');
        reverseError.textContent = 'Enter valid tons, depth, and supplier weight to estimate coverage.';
      } else {
        output('trap-known-coverage', (known * 2000 / densityValue * 324 / depthValue).toFixed(1));
        reverseError.textContent = '';
      }
    }
    [...modes, length, width, areaInput, depth, extra, density, knownTons].forEach(field => field.addEventListener('input', calculate));
    calculate();
  }
  initTrapCalculator();
</script>

<style>
